# TEMPLATE ONLY - never put real values in this file.
#
# This is a public repository (github.com/GhostKellz/arch). The previous
# restic.env was tracked here with placeholder values, one careless copy away
# from publishing live credentials. restic.env is now gitignored; this
# .example file is the only version that should ever be committed.
#
# Real credentials are NOT stored as a plaintext env file at all. They are
# sealed with systemd-creds (TPM2 + host key) into /etc/credstore.encrypted/
# and loaded by the units via LoadCredentialEncrypted=. See README.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Repository password  ->  /etc/credstore.encrypted/restic-repo-password
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate:  openssl rand -base64 48
# ESCROW THIS IN YOUR PASSWORD MANAGER BEFORE THE FIRST BACKUP RUNS.
# TPM-sealed credentials are machine-bound: if this host dies, the sealed copy
# dies with it, and the password manager copy is the only way into the repo.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Wasabi credentials  ->  /etc/credstore.encrypted/restic-aws-credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Written in AWS shared-credentials format, NOT as env vars:
#
#   [default]
#   aws_access_key_id = <sub-user key>
#   aws_secret_access_key = <sub-user secret>
#
# Must be a Wasabi SUB-USER scoped to the ck-arch bucket only.
# Root account keys cannot be scoped and would give this workstation
# reach into client-data buckets. See policy.json.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Repository (not secret, set via Environment= in the unit)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RESTIC_REPOSITORY=s3:https://s3.wasabisys.com/ck-arch
